
<p>Jonathan O’Connell, Sarah Blaskey, and Aaron Schaffer, reporting for The Washington Post (<a href="https://apple.news/AblrTEVS5ROWp089jXUp2oQ">News+ link</a>; <a href="https://archive.is/20261015145817/https://www.washingtonpost.com/investigations/2026/10/15/secret-documents-reveal-donors-behind-330-million-trumps-ballroom/">Archive.today link</a>):</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>A list obtained by The Washington Post reveals all the major
donors to President Donald Trump’s White House ballroom project,
including previously undisclosed contributors who have benefited
from regulatory decisions, government contracts and, in one case,
a pardon for a family member.</p>

<p>Tech giants Nvidia and Apple each committed $25 million, the top
contribution amount, the list shows. The Adelson family, one of
the GOP’s largest donor dynasties, committed that sum as well, as
did billionaire Charles Ergen, co-founder and chairman of
telecommunications company Dish Network.</p>

<p>Absent from the list is Trump himself, who has said that he is
helping to pay for the project. “It’s being paid for a hundred
percent by me and some friends of mine,” he <a href="https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-prior-meeting-with-secretary-general-mark-rutte-the-north-atlantic-treaty-2">said</a> last year,
days after <a href="https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefings-statements/2025/07/the-white-house-announces-white-house-ballroom-construction-to-begin/">demolition</a> began on the old East Wing.</p>

<p>The White House publicly identified 37 people, companies and
foundations as donors last October, without revealing the amount
of their individual gifts.</p>

<p>The list obtained by The Post, an internal spreadsheet used to
track contributions for the fundraising effort, contains the
source, date and amount of all 67 donations above $10,000 recorded
as of late last month. It shows a total of $333 million from
private donors, aligning with the administration’s public
statements. Additional records, also internal to the fundraising
effort, provide more detail about the donations and indicate that
at least $77 million came from donors who requested anonymity.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>In addition to being a shameful display of abject corruption, it’s also a shameful act of support for <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/03/29/upshot/white-house-ballroom.html?unlocked_article_code=1.I1E.hf2j.MJyjcOInOeEA">horrendous architecture</a>. This entire edifice ought to be destroyed — I won’t vote for any Democratic candidate in the 2028 primary who doesn’t pledge to knock all this Trump cack to the ground in January 2029. That’s table stakes.</p>

